[Music]  this hospital in the colombian coastal  town tumaco  wasn't prepared for the pandemic the  intensive care unit was only built after  covert 19 had reached the city  and infected thousands  far from the capital bogota the  population here has never been the focus  of government decisions  now it's paying for the lack of health  infrastructure  like many other remote parts of the  country  the coronavirus is riskier for  indigenous communities  lack of health services and clean water  poverty  keeping variants out of rural canada has  been difficult  but adults in remote parts are getting  priority access to the vaccine  aboriginal and torres strait islander  people prevented widespread infections  in australia with their own travel  restrictions  their vaccine rollout is getting  underway indigenous groups are also  among the first to be getting the jab in  new zealand the us  canada and brazil but things don't look  so good in colombia  andres rojas has been working for a year  in the intensive care unit  of the saint andrews hospital in tumaco  it's the only hospital in the far  southwest of colombia  a year ago the clinic didn't have any  intensive care bids  things changed quickly when the covet-19  pandemic began  at the start of the first outbreak we  had more than 2500 patients  21 of them died and 17 were transferred  to another hospital  hospital is one of many provincial  clinics in colombia  that opened up intensive care units for  the first time during the pandemic  it was only after the pandemic hit that  we could even think about setting up an  intensive care station  at the moment it provides 13 units with  monitors breathing devices  electric beds and staff who we trained  ourselves  according to dr rojas some funding came  from the government  but most of it came from private  initiatives local businesses were  especially generous with their donations  tomoko is dependent on the tourist  industry  this is an advertisement from better  times the location is the pacific coast  next to ecuador's border around 1 000  kilometers  from the capital of bogota tourists come  here from around the world  despite the location's popularity  medical infrastructure was largely  ignored by politicians in the capital of  bogota  before the hospital had 38 oxygen tanks  imagine that trying to get through a  pandemic with 38 tanks of oxygen  thanks to donations from local business  totaling 400 000 pesos  we could set up the hospital with a  liquid oxygen tank  this helps a little but the second wave  of infections is now bringing a shortage  of medicine  no one here can think about vaccines  against covet 19  the staff are approaching burnout  you can feel their tiredness some staff  members have fainted from exhaustion  their working hours are very very long  at the beginning of march 117 000  vaccine doses were delivered to bogota  these were meant to reach 50 million  colombians  but nobody in tumaco believes that they  will get a single dose  juan mario diaz is a research fellow in  the department of politics and  international relations at the  university of sheffield  why is the situation so difficult in  some of colombia's marginalized  communities  well to understand the situation of  these marginalized communities it's  important to  take into consideration what former  government called  the historical depth of colombian state  with  rural areas which basically can be  summarized in four aspects the first one  is the  the development gap which refers to  rural areas not having basic services  like water electricity roads hospitals  or schools the second refers to a  social inequality and lung concentration  according to oxfam  uh 66 percent of productive life  la land in colombia is in the hands of  0.4 percent of the total population  the third one is the problem of the lack  of states presence in some areas as  some of them are still under control of  illegal groups and the fourth  perhaps the most worrying is the que  the victims of the question the question  victim sorry  which according to the 2013 report of  the center  of historical memory indicates that  more than 200 000 people were killed in  the last 15 years  and more than 5 million of colombians  were displaced  from rural areas so this is a a picture  that  indicates why the situation is so  complicated for this  communities juan mario can you tell me  what happened to the peace process  surely that has improved the situation  in some way  well the peace process is an important  landmark in colombia history  uh initially was portrayed as as a way  of  bringing to an end 50 years of war uh  which was a narrative that really  strongly appealed to the international  community and this is why the  this process was surrounded by the  international organizations  but what what is interesting is what  happened in colombia during  the four years that the conversations in  havana  occurred a series of national  and regional forums were convened by the  negotiating parties which allow people  to discuss  the points of the peace agenda basically  people get involved in discussing  agrarian reform political participation  the question of victims  uh transitional justice and the  substitution of  illegal crops which gave us the sense  as a as a colombians that the peace  process was a great opportunity to  transform  peacefully all of these social problems  that we have historically had in the  country  so that'll bring a lot of hope but at  the same time the  the the no referendum the victory  the victory of the no camp of the  referendum plus the lack of  implementation of the process is a  matter of concern as  we see all the high hopes of the  national population being  met with um with no compliance  and delusion okay so many social  problems remain  especially for afro-colombian groups i  believe how is the pandemic affecting  their situation  uh i think the pandemia has just made  more apparent  a structural problems in the pacific  coast  and in some rural areas  just to give you an idea just before the  pandemia  40 of public hospitals were closed  and and so the former government  declared a state of social emergency  when the vadim came uh hospitals in  rural areas were completely unprepared  to deal with that  and well basically the lack of resources  and the lack of  agency from some uh communities were  just uh  something that they really struggled to  to deal with  but what is more concerning for for  these communities  is not just the virus but also the  re-emergence of violence  which allow us to see the whole  situation of a syndemia which is the  combination of  the pandemia plus structural elements  that  impinge on a  bringing solutions to people and to  dealing with the problem effectively  so what's the best way to tackle the big  divide in colombia and ensure these  people get treated or vaccinated  uh i think the first thing we need to  think about  is how to de-politicize the  the way that anemia is addressing the  country we cannot think this is a  political problem but as a social  problem  as a health problem so i think the  government need to  listen as well more what people is  saying from the territory from  what the local uh the local  administrations are  these messages that the local  administrations are sending to the to  the local  to the central government in order to  get better results in the way that  colombia is dealing with this okay we'll  have to leave it there juan mahario diaz  thank you very much for being on the  show today  thank you very much if you've got a  question about the coronavirus feel free  to get in contact with our science  correspondent  derek williams  if you are asymptomatic and don't know  you have copied 19  is it dangerous to be vaccinated  the short answer to this appears to be  no  but it's a pretty complicated question  so of course  there's a little more to it than that um  current advice  from the u.s centers for disease control  focuses mostly  on whether or not people who've had the  disease and recovered  can be safely vaccinated afterwards the  answer to that  is a clear yes however the cdc  is far less clear about people who might  have an active asymptomatic infection at  the moment they get a shot  the implication though is certainly that  there's no reason to think  that vaccination would pose a danger  that's because the guidelines do say  there's no need to test negative for  infection  before you're vaccinated they also say  that even if you've been exposed  and have been tested and are awaiting  results if you live somewhere  like a rest home you should go ahead and  be vaccinated as long as you don't have  any symptoms i'm sure the guidelines  wouldn't say either one of those things  if there were suspected dangers  for people with asymptomatic infections  being  given the shot and that the cdc would  adopt a much more  cautious approach but it's important  also to remember something else at the  same time  if you know you've been exposed and are  in quarantine  but aren't showing any symptoms don't go  get vaccinated  until your quarantine period has passed  because  you might be an asymptomatic carrier and  even though we've established the  vaccine doesn't pose a threat to you in  that case  you could still pose a potential threat  to people who are vaccinating you  so wait out your couple of weeks just in  case  to protect them  and just briefly the world health  organization says the astrazeneca  vaccine rollout should continue for now  some countries have stopped it over  reports of blood clots  finally we leave you with images from  sao paulo brazil's biggest city  now more of a ghost town the usually  bustling mega city of  21 million people went into curfew this  week to try to stop the coronavirus from  spreading  several brazilian cities running out of  hospital beds as cases  rise and the crisis worsens  thanks for watching  you

